(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In people who inject drugs, what share of tricuspid valve endocarditis is caused by Staphylococcus aureus?","choices":["Under 5%","10 to 20%","30 to 40%","80 to 90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs, Staphylococcus aureus, a skin organism, causes more than 60% of all endocarditis and 80-90% of cases in which the tricuspid valve is infected. The tricuspid figure is the higher of the two numbers. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which disorder in the differential of infective endocarditis causes myalgia with elevated inflammatory markers?","choices":["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Atrial myxoma","Tuberculosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymyalgia rheumatica appears in the autoimmune and rheumatologic differential of infective endocarditis because it causes myalgia with elevated inflammatory markers (erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein). That constitutional picture can mimic endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the most likely complication of fungal infective endocarditis?","choices":["Isolated first-degree heart block","Spontaneous resolution within days","Painful nodules on the finger pads","Large peripheral artery emboli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In fungal infective endocarditis, the most likely complication is embolism and occlusion of larger peripheral arteries from embolization. Whatever the host, a fungal organism on culture means large-artery embolic events are the complication to anticipate. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"In a high-risk patient, which oral event does not call for endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Bleeding from lip trauma","Periodontal scaling","A tooth extraction","A gum biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding from trauma to the lips or oral mucosa is on the list that does not require endocarditis prophylaxis, along with routine anesthetic injections through noninfected tissue, dental radiographs and orthodontic placement or adjustment. No procedure is manipulating the gums or perforating the mucosa. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A perivalvular abscess that extends into the upper ventricular septum causes which complication?","choices":["Rupture of a mycotic aneurysm","Septic emboli to the lungs","Interruption of the conduction system","Pericarditis from epicardial spread"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A perivalvular abscess is infection that extends beyond the valve leaflets into myocardial tissue. When it extends into the upper ventricular septum, it interrupts the conduction system; when it extends into the epicardium, it causes pericarditis instead. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"The HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group of endocarditis organisms comes from which source?","choices":["The upper respiratory tract","The genitourinary tract","The skin surface","The lower bowel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organisms come from the upper respiratory tract: Haemophilus aphrophilus, Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans, Cardiobacterium hominis, Eikenella corrodens and Kingella species. They are a subacute, native valve cause. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In infants, which complications are common because septic emboli are frequent?","choices":["Heart failure from aortic stenosis","Splinter hemorrhages and clubbing","Osteomyelitis, meningitis and pneumonia","Glomerulonephritis and Roth spots"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Septic emboli are common in neonates and infants, producing osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia, and neurologic signs such as seizures, hemiparesis and apnea are frequent. Pediatric endocarditis overall carries a higher septic embolism rate than adult disease, up to about 40%. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which is the most common indication for surgery in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Fever on the third hospital day","Heart failure from valvular dysfunction","Any vegetation seen on echocardiogram","A first positive blood culture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure due to valvular dysfunction is the most common indication for surgery in infective endocarditis. The other indications are infection not resolving with appropriate antibiotic therapy, abscess and fungal infection, which is difficult to cure with medications alone. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why is fungal infective endocarditis an indication for surgery?","choices":["It is hard to cure with medication alone","It spreads to the pericardium","Antifungals cannot be given intravenously","Fungi do not grow in blood cultures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fungal infection is an indication for surgery because it is difficult to cure with medications alone. It sits alongside heart failure due to valvular dysfunction (the most common indication), infection not resolving with appropriate antibiotic therapy and abscess. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which description fits Janeway lesions?","choices":["White-centered hemorrhages seen in the retina","Thin dark lines under the proximal nail plate","Painless hemorrhagic macules on palms and soles","Painful nodules on the pads of fingers and toes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Janeway lesions are raised or macular hemorrhagic lesions on the soles of the feet and palms of the hands, caused by septic emboli and NONTENDER. They are associated with acute infective endocarditis, and their painless quality separates them from Osler nodes. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"When the presentation suggests acute endocarditis, how quickly should the first cultures be drawn?","choices":["No cultures until antibiotics have begun","Two cultures within the first 1 to 2 hours","One culture at the end of the first day","Three cultures spread over three days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When the presentation suggests acute endocarditis, 2 blood cultures are drawn within the first 1-2 hours, instead of the usual 3 sets within 24 hours. Empiric antibiotics start only after the cultures are drawn, so the short window keeps therapy from being delayed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What damages non-valvular endocardium enough for infective endocarditis to take hold there?","choices":["Low blood flow during sleep","Lipid plaque deposition","Pericardial fluid pressure","Blood jets or foreign bodies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Besides the heart valves (the most common site), the low-pressure side of a ventricular septal defect and intracardiac devices, infective endocarditis affects endocardium damaged by aberrant jets of blood or by foreign bodies. That damaged lining is where microorganisms invade and colonize. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In a patient at high risk, which procedure does not require endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Tonsillectomy","Root canal","Colonoscopy","Tooth extraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal and genitourinary procedures, including cystoscopy, upper endoscopy and colonoscopy, do not require endocarditis prophylaxis. Prophylaxis is reserved for manipulation of gingival tissue or the periapical region of the teeth, perforation of the oral mucosa, or invasive respiratory procedures that incise mucosa. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which complete blood count pattern is expected in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Normal white count with polycythemia","Eosinophilia with macrocytic anemia","Leukocytosis with normocytic anemia","Leukopenia with microcytic anemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The complete blood count with differential in infective endocarditis shows leukocytosis and anemia of chronic disease with a normal mean corpuscular volume, that is, a normocytic anemia. Alongside it, urinalysis may show hematuria, proteinuria and pyuria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Where are splinter hemorrhages found?","choices":["In the retina, with a white center","On the hard palate and conjunctiva","On the pads of the fingers and toes","Under the fingernails and toenails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Splinter hemorrhages are found under the fingernails and toenails, in the proximal portion of the nail plate, and they can be painful. They are listed among the embolic complications of endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which course is typical of subacute infective endocarditis?","choices":["Low fevers, malaise and weight loss over weeks","High fevers and toxicity within a few days","Sudden collapse with no preceding illness","Painful joints that migrate every few hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Subacute infective endocarditis has an indolent course: lower fevers, generalized malaise and weight loss occurring over weeks or months. It is slow to cause structural cardiac damage, if at all, rarely metastasizes and is gradually progressive unless complicated by a major embolic event or a ruptured mycotic aneurysm. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"On which side of a ventricular septal defect does infective endocarditis develop?","choices":["The high-pressure side","Both sides equally","Neither side; the valves instead","The low-pressure side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Infective endocarditis can affect the low-pressure side of a ventricular septal defect, alongside the heart valves (most common), endocardium damaged by aberrant jets of blood or foreign bodies, and intracardiac devices. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which organism causes the largest share of health care-associated native valve endocarditis?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Enterococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus causes about 52% of health care-associated native valve endocarditis, compared with about 28% of community-acquired cases, and methicillin resistance is common among these health care-associated strains. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why is clindamycin no longer recommended for endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Risk of Clostridioides difficile","It causes QT prolongation","It misses viridans streptococci","It must be given for weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is no longer recommended for endocarditis prophylaxis because of the risk of Clostridioides difficile infection. Penicillin-allergic patients now receive azithromycin or clarithromycin, doxycycline, or a cephalosporin if there is no history of anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"In Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis, mortality is highest in which group?","choices":["Native valves, non-injecting","Children with heart disease","Prosthetic valve infection","People who inject drugs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus, the most lethal endocarditis organism, carries about 50% mortality in prosthetic valve endocarditis, compared with about 30% in people who do not inject drugs and about 5% in people who inject drugs. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which organism belongs to the fastidious gram-negative group of endocarditis organisms from the upper respiratory tract?","choices":["Streptococcus gallolyticus","Eikenella corrodens","Enterococcus faecalis","Staphylococcus lugdunensis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Eikenella corrodens is the E of the HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group of upper respiratory tract organisms: Haemophilus aphrophilus, Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans, Cardiobacterium hominis, Eikenella corrodens and Kingella species. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"For how long after congenital heart disease repair with prosthetic material or a device is prophylaxis warranted?","choices":["The first 6 weeks","The first 6 months","The first 5 years","For life"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The first 6 months after congenital heart disease repair with prosthetic material or a device is a high-risk pediatric condition that warrants prophylaxis before qualifying procedures. After that, the repair stays high risk only if residual defects lie adjacent to the prosthetic material. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Without treatment, how quickly does acute infective endocarditis progress to death?","choices":["Within hours","Within weeks","Over several months","Over many years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If untreated, acute infective endocarditis progresses to death within weeks. A virulent, invasive organism causes a hectically febrile illness that rapidly damages cardiac structures and seeds extracardiac sites, with rapid onset of high fevers and systemic toxicity typically over 3-10 days. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are Roth spots?","choices":["Thin dark lines under the nails","Painless macules on palms and soles","White-centered retinal hemorrhages","Painful nodules on the finger pads"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Roth spots are white-centered hemorrhages in the retina caused by vasculitis affecting the small arteries of the retina. They are the eye finding of endocarditis, distinct from the nail, skin and finger-pad findings. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which single positive blood culture is excluded from the microbiologic minor criterion as a common contaminant?","choices":["Streptococcus gallolyticus","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Single positive cultures for coagulase-negative staphylococci and diphtheroids are excluded because they are common culture contaminants, as are single cultures of organisms that rarely cause endocarditis, such as gram-negative bacilli. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which combination of modified Duke criteria establishes definite infective endocarditis?","choices":["Two minor criteria","One major and three minor","One major and one minor","Three minor criteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Definite infective endocarditis is documented by two major criteria, one major plus three minor criteria, or five minor criteria. One major with three minor is the middle of those three combinations, so it establishes a definite diagnosis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What causes infective endocarditis?","choices":["Immune complexes alone, with no organism","Coronary plaque rupture with thrombosis","Fibrous scarring of the pericardial sac","Microbes colonizing the endocardium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Infective endocarditis is caused by invasion and colonization of the endocardial structures by microorganisms, with resulting inflammation. The infective vegetation that forms is a mass of platelets, fibrin, micro-colonies of micro-organisms and inflammatory cells. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"By which route are antibiotics for infective endocarditis given?","choices":["Inhaled","Parenterally","Orally","Topically"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endocarditis antibiotics should be given parenterally, as intravenous therapy with hospital admission, close monitoring of labs, renal function and cardiovascular status, and a multidisciplinary team. Therapy must also be bactericidal and prolonged, typically 4-6 weeks from the first negative culture. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which organism is a classic cause of subacute infective endocarditis?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Beta-hemolytic streptococci","Candida species"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The subacute organisms are viridans streptococci, enterococci, coagulase-negative staphylococci and the HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group. Viridans streptococci come from the oral cavity and are the classic subacute cause. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"When does bronchoscopy call for endocarditis prophylaxis in a high-risk patient?","choices":["Not even when a biopsy is taken","When sedation is planned","When the mucosa is incised, as for biopsy","Every bronchoscopy, whatever is done"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bronchoscopy is on the list that does not require endocarditis prophylaxis unless it involves incision of the mucosa, such as a biopsy. Invasive respiratory procedures that incise or biopsy mucosa, such as tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy, do qualify. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
